{
  "0": "Feature construction: multiply each input embedding by 1 + 0.1*min(log1p(c), log(4))/log(4), where c counts earlier occurrences of that item within the supplied history prefix. This bounded repeat indicator may distinguish sustained interests from isolated purchases without suppressing rare items. Compute causally and identically during training and prediction; preserve embedding parameters, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer.",
  "1": "Network architecture: apply learned layer normalization to the GRU candidate-state preactivation before tanh, leaving reset and update gates unchanged. Stabilizing candidate activations may improve recurrent optimization across categories with different interaction densities, with little additional computation. Preserve hidden width, depth, input features, embeddings, history selection, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer; serialize the normalization parameters in each category's single checkpoint.",
  "2": "Interest aggregation: replace the current readout with normalized exponential pooling over existing valid GRU states, using weights proportional to 2^(-d/5), where d is distance from the latest valid position. A five-interaction half-life may retain useful recent interests while reducing sensitivity to the final purchase. Mask padding and preserve history selection, embeddings, recurrent backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer. Train and predict with the identical readout.",
  "3": "Training strategy: change only the learning-rate schedule: warm up linearly from 0.1 times the existing learning rate to its full value over five epochs, then cosine-decay to 0.1 times that value by epoch 200. Gradual startup and smaller late updates may reduce validation oscillation without increasing per-epoch cost. Keep optimizer type, batch size, regularization, model, loss, validation checkpoint selection, early stopping, seeds and epoch cap unchanged."
}
